  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 224 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nRain could occur again overnight Saturday into Sunday as another\nshortwave/MCS feature works its way east through southern KS.\nNot every model has the wave making its way east, but if it can,\nthere is the possibility for thunderstorms in the same area as\nearlier in the day. This wave has a bit more lift associated\nwith it and we have the chance for rain at 30-45%. SPC hasn't\noutlooked this area outside of a general thunder risk for Sunday.\nThis isn't to say a Marginal risk wouldn't be issued, we'll \njust have to monitor how the convection plays out today and \nassess whether or not there are any leftover boundaries from \nthis morning's convection, or if it stabilizes the atmosphere \nand we get nothing overnight. \n\nA stationary front hangs out over the northern part of our CWA\nfor most of the week and brings off and on rain chances for\ncentral MO. Blame the high pressure system sitting over the\nsouth for our area missing out on all the rain. Once that ridge\nbegins to move east later in the week, rain chances increase to\n30-40% by the end of the week. Unfortunately, not a lot of\ntemperature changes or any signs of a cooldown coming this \nweek. We do start to creep into the 80s towards the end of the \nwork week, but it won't exactly feel like fall quite yet.",
